---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { canonicalFoundationCommands } from '../data/products.ts';

const title = 'How Coven works · OpenCoven';
const description = 'How the current Coven runtime coordinates local agent work through durable sessions, shared claims, evidence, and human-approved protected actions.';
---

<SiteLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/how-it-works">
  <section class="site-shell hero" id="top">
    <div class="hero__copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Current runtime behavior</p>
      <h1>The local layer your agent sessions share.</h1>
      <p class="hero__lede">
        Coven sits at the local project boundary. Your coding harness still reasons and
        edits; Coven owns the durable session state, claims, execution records, and guard
        decisions those sessions need to coordinate safely.
      </p>
      <div class="button-row">
        <a class="action action--primary" data-oc-primitive="action" data-oc-state="idle" href="/quickstart">Start locally</a>
        <a class="action" data-oc-primitive="action" data-oc-state="idle" href="/protocol">See the protocol direction</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <aside class="evidence-card">
      <span class="status status--available" data-oc-primitive="status-indicator" data-oc-state="success">Available today</span>
      <h2 style="font-size: var(--oc-type-2); margin-top: var(--oc-space-4)">Scope of this page</h2>
      <p class="muted">This route describes current Coven runtime behavior. Familiar identity, continuity profiles, and broader cross-surface authority semantics live on the protocol route and carry their own maturity labels.</p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="flow-heading">
    <div class="site-shell">
      <div class="band__intro">
        <h2 id="flow-heading">One project, shared local evidence.</h2>
        <p>The runtime does not make every session one agent. It gives independent sessions a common place to state intent, preserve records, and encounter explicit authority boundaries.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="proof-sequence">
        <article class="proof-step"><div class="proof-step__body"><h3>A session enters a project.</h3><p>Coven binds work to the current local project and restores durable session context rather than treating every terminal process as the whole system.</p></div></article>
        <article class="proof-step"><div class="proof-step__body"><h3>Intent becomes inspectable.</h3><p>Claims and records make overlapping work visible, so two sessions do not need to coordinate through prompt folklore or silent last-writer-wins behavior.</p></div></article>
        <article class="proof-step"><div class="proof-step__body"><h3>Protected work can wait.</h3><p>Guarded local operations can pause for human approval instead of inferring authority merely because an agent has context or filesystem access.</p></div></article>
        <article class="proof-step"><div class="proof-step__body"><h3>The record survives the terminal.</h3><p>Durable runtime state makes claims, sessions, and evidence inspectable after an individual shell or harness process exits.</p></div></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="boundary-heading">
    <div class="site-shell">
      <div class="band__intro">
        <h2 id="boundary-heading">What Coven owns—and what it does not.</h2>
        <p>Keeping these boundaries explicit prevents the runtime from quietly becoming a second identity protocol, orchestration plane, or product UI authority.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-grid">
        <article class="concept-card"><h3>Runtime authority</h3><p>Durable session state, claims, local execution, persistence, and enforcement at the project boundary.</p></article>
        <article class="concept-card"><h3>Not identity definition</h3><p>The Familiar Contract defines the governed familiar self; Coven consumes authorized identity rather than redefining it.</p></article>
        <article class="concept-card"><h3>Not orchestration policy</h3><p>Psyche owns task, lane, lease, approval, receipt, and recovery protocol semantics across orchestrated work.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="start-heading">
    <div class="site-shell">
      <div class="band__intro"><h2 id="start-heading">Verify it from one project.</h2><p>The canonical foundation remains deliberately small.</p></div>
      <div class="command-block">
        {canonicalFoundationCommands.map((item, index) => <div class="command-row"><span>{index + 1}</span><code>{item.command}</code></div>)}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
